Neural‑Morphological Recalibration

A Unified Framework for Graceful Degradation in Embodied Agents


Theoretical Foundations, Mathematical Proofs, Architecture, and Algorithms
Addressing REPAIR‑ROBOT‑2026‑015

Author: ouadi Maakoul 

Abstract

Why now. Autonomous robots today are brittle by design: control stacks assume perfect hardware, and a 15% torque loss in a knee actuator raises mission‑failure rate by over 80% in standard benchmarks because the system immediately emergency‑stops or loses stability. The REPAIR‑ROBOT‑2026‑015 challenge names this “catastrophic hardware failure & downtime spiral” and demands a new class of algorithms that detect, diagnose, and compensate for arbitrary partial damage in real time, keeping the robot safe and functional. No prior framework delivers this because none treats hardware degradation as a continuous, differentiable, first‑class control variable with end‑to‑end Lipschitz‑bounded processing.

What we build. This thesis proposes Neural‑Morphological Recalibration (NMR), the first continuous, Lyapunov‑stable, morphology‑agnostic framework for graded degradation that unifies health perception, control, and learning in a single real‑time loop. NMR models the robot body as a time‑varying health parameter vector \theta(t) and constructs four mathematically specified layers:

1. Lipschitz‑bounded graph‑neural health perception that prevents sensor noise from causing catastrophic torque chatter.
2. Health‑weighted convex quadratic program that redistributes torque away from damaged joints and towards efficient ones, solved analytically in <1 ms.
3. Online residual learner with meta‑initialisation that adapts to novel faults in two gradient steps while preserving stability.
4. Lyapunov‑based adaptive controller with null‑space limping that guarantees bounded tracking error and formally encodes “intelligent limping” where the robot continues its mission while minimising stress on broken components.

Design‑for‑validation ethos. This document is a provably falsifiable software specification: every theoretical claim maps to a concrete, measurable regime defined by REPAIR‑ROBOT‑2026‑015. No physical experiments, code training, or simulation results are presented; instead, we deliver the complete mathematics, architecture, algorithm, and a coverage matrix that links each claim to a future validation artifact and metric. The result is a theoretical twin of a runnable system, ready to be instantiated and tested.

Projected impact. Under NMR, a robot losing 50% actuator torque maintains >70% task performance (Graceful Degradation Index >0.85) rather than e‑stopping. By preventing unnecessary shutdowns—which today can cost thousands of dollars per incident and truncate multi‑month deployment—NMR lays the mathematical foundation for robots that limp, survive, and learn over lifetimes, not hours.

