3. System Model and Continuous Health Representation

3.1 Dynamics with Degradation

We model the robot as a nonlinear, control‑affine system with a time‑varying health parameter vector \theta(t)\in\Theta\subset\mathbb{R}^p:

\dot{x} = f(x) + g(x) u + \delta(x, \theta(t))
\tag{1}

where x\in\mathbb{R}^n is the state (joint positions and velocities), u\in\mathbb{R}^m is the control input (torques), and \delta is a bounded, smooth perturbation capturing the effect of degradation on unactuated dynamics. At nominal health \theta_0, the system is fully actuated and controllable. The vector \theta(t) may drift slowly (wear, thermal buildup) or undergo abrupt jumps (seizure, breakage).

For control design, we assume the dynamics can be written in the standard regressor form:

M(q)\ddot{q} + C(q,\dot{q})\dot{q} + g(q) = \tau + Y(q,\dot{q},\dot{q}_r,\ddot{q}_r)\theta
\tag{2}

where M(q) is the inertia matrix, C the Coriolis matrix, g the gravity vector, and Y a known regressor matrix that uses a reference velocity \dot{q}_r and acceleration \ddot{q}_r (to be defined later). This parametrization is standard for rigid‑body robots and holds for joint‑level degradation that affects inertial, frictional, or elastic parameters.

3.2 Distributed Health Monitoring

Every joint/actuator node i is instrumented with diagnostics: actual torque \tau_i, commanded torque, temperature T_i, vibration power spectral density, tendon strain \varepsilon_i (from hyper‑redundant tendons), and tactile skin pressure maps. From these, a local health scalar h_i(t)\in[0,1] is computed by a deterministic, normalized mapping:

h_i(t) = \sigma\!\left( \sum_{k} \alpha_k \phi_k(\text{diag}_i(t)) \right)
\tag{3}

where \sigma(\cdot)=\frac{1}{1+e^{-(\cdot)}} (sigmoid) and \phi_k are bounded feature extractors (|\phi_k|\le 1). The coefficients \alpha_k are fixed by design or pre‑trained offline. The crucial property is that h_i is Lipschitz continuous in the raw diagnostics.

The robot’s kinematic and physical connectivity is encoded as a directed graph \mathcal{G}=(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{E}) with nodes \mathcal{V}=\{1,\dots,N\} for joints/links and edges for mechanical coupling. Each node i carries a feature vector \mathbf{f}_i = [h_i, \dot{h}_i, \tau_i^{\text{err}}, \dots]^\top \in \mathbb{R}^{d_f}.

3.3 Graph Neural Health Embedding with Lipschitz Guarantee

A multi‑layer graph neural network (GNN) F_\phi with parameters \phi computes a global health context vector \mathbf{h}^{\text{global}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_h}:

\mathbf{z}_i^{(0)} = \mathbf{f}_i;\qquad
\mathbf{z}_i^{(l+1)} = \text{ReLU}\!\left( W^{(l)} \mathbf{z}_i^{(l)} + \sum_{j\in\mathcal{N}(i)} \alpha_{ij}^{(l)} \mathbf{z}_j^{(l)} \right), \quad l=0,\dots,L-1
\tag{4}

\mathbf{h}^{\text{global}} = \text{READOUT}\!\left( \{\mathbf{z}_i^{(L)}\}_{i=1}^N \right)

The readout is a permutation‑invariant mean pooling followed by a linear map.

Intuition for Lemma 1: For the health‑based controller to be safe, small sensor perturbations must not cause wild torque jumps. The entire path from raw diagnostics to the global health embedding \mathbf{h}^{\text{global}} must be Lipschitz‑bounded. We enforce this through spectral normalisation and normalised aggregation.

Lemma 1 (Lipschitz Health Embedding). Assume each weight matrix satisfies \|W^{(l)}\|_2 \le 1, the ReLU activation is 1‑Lipschitz, the aggregation weights \alpha_{ij}^{(l)} are normalised (\sum_j \alpha_{ij}^{(l)} \le 1), and the readout is 1‑Lipschitz. Then the overall mapping \mathbf{f}\mapsto\mathbf{h}^{\text{global}} is L_h-Lipschitz with L_h \le 2^L. For typical depth L=3, L_h \le 8.

Proof. For any two input feature sets \mathbf{f},\tilde{\mathbf{f}}, let \mathbf{z}_i^{(l)},\tilde{\mathbf{z}}_i^{(l)} be the corresponding node embeddings. At layer 0, \|\mathbf{z}_i^{(0)} - \tilde{\mathbf{z}}_i^{(0)}\| = \|\mathbf{f}_i - \tilde{\mathbf{f}}_i\|. Inductive step:

\begin{aligned}
\|\mathbf{z}_i^{(l+1)} - \tilde{\mathbf{z}}_i^{(l+1)}\| &\le \left\| \text{ReLU}(W\mathbf{z}_i^{(l)} + \sum\alpha_{ij}\mathbf{z}_j^{(l)}) - \text{ReLU}(W\tilde{\mathbf{z}}_i^{(l)} + \sum\alpha_{ij}\tilde{\mathbf{z}}_j^{(l)}) \right\| \\
&\le \|W(\mathbf{z}_i^{(l)}-\tilde{\mathbf{z}}_i^{(l)}) + \sum\alpha_{ij}(\mathbf{z}_j^{(l)}-\tilde{\mathbf{z}}_j^{(l)})\| \\
&\le \|\mathbf{z}_i^{(l)}-\tilde{\mathbf{z}}_i^{(l)}\| + \sum\alpha_{ij}\|\mathbf{z}_j^{(l)}-\tilde{\mathbf{z}}_j^{(l)}\|.
\end{aligned}

Summing over all i and using the normalisation of \alpha_{ij}, the total embedding distance at layer l+1 is at most twice the maximum distance at layer l. Thus after L layers, the amplification factor is \le 2^L. The readout adds at most a factor of 1, giving L_h \le 2^L. ∎

This Lipschitz bound is critical for safety: it limits how much sensor noise or adversarial spoofing can perturb the health perception, preventing runaway torque oscillations in the downstream controller.

