5. Online Residual Learning for Unknown Degradation Dynamics

5.1 Control Law Structure

Even with optimal torque redistribution, unmodeled effects of degradation (changed friction, compliance, inertia distribution) cause tracking errors. We augment a nominal control policy \pi_{\text{nom}}(x) with a residual neural network \Delta\pi_\psi:

u = \pi_{\text{nom}}(x) + \Delta\pi_\psi(x, \mathbf{h}^{\text{global}})
\tag{8}

The nominal policy is optimal for \theta=\theta_0 and may be, for example, a computed‑torque controller or a model‑predictive controller designed on the healthy dynamics. \Delta\pi_\psi is a feed‑forward multi‑layer perceptron with parameters \psi.

5.2 Online Training Objective

The residual network is trained online to minimise:

\mathcal{L}(\psi) = \frac{1}{2}\| x - x_d \|_Q^2 + \frac{\lambda}{2} \| \Delta\pi_\psi \|_2^2
\tag{9}

The regularisation discourages large corrective actions that could destabilise the system.

5.3 Efficient Update via Learned Inverse Dynamics

To compute gradients without backpropagating through the true physics, we maintain an approximate inverse dynamics model \hat{f}^{-1} that predicts the next state given current state and action. The model is updated continuously using a sliding window of recent (x_t, u_t, x_{t+1}) tuples via recursive Ridge regression. The gradient of the loss w.r.t. \psi is approximated as:

\nabla_\psi \mathcal{L} \approx \left( \frac{\partial \hat{f}^{-1}}{\partial u} \right)^\top (x_{t+1} - x_d) \frac{\partial \Delta\pi_\psi}{\partial \psi}
\tag{10}

where the Jacobian \partial \hat{f}^{-1}/\partial u is obtained from the linearised inverse model. This one‑step approximation is computationally cheap and enables weight updates every 50 ms with a single step of the Adam optimiser (learning rate 10^{-3}).

5.4 Meta‑Learning for Rapid Adaptation

To further accelerate adaptation to unforeseen faults, the parameters \psi can be initialised via model‑agnostic meta‑learning (MAML) on a distribution of simulated degradation scenarios. After meta‑training, only 1–2 gradient steps are needed to specialise to a new fault, limiting the adaptation latency to <5 ms.

5.5 Stability Safeguard

The residual network is constrained to be Lipschitz with respect to its inputs by spectral normalisation of its weight matrices. Together with the bounded health embedding, this prevents runaway corrections. Additionally, a watchdog monitors the norm of \Delta\pi_\psi; if it exceeds a pre‑defined threshold for more than 200 ms, the residual is disabled and the system reverts to the Lyapunov‑backed adaptive controller (Chapter 6), guaranteeing boundedness.

