6. Lyapunov‑Based Adaptive Control and Stability Proof

6.1 Composite Adaptive Controller

We design a control law that explicitly estimates the true health parameter vector \theta. The nominal feedback linearization is augmented with an adaptive term:

\tau = M(q)\ddot{q}_r + C(q,\dot{q})\dot{q}_r + g(q) - K_d s - Y(q,\dot{q},\dot{q}_r,\ddot{q}_r) \hat{\theta}
\tag{11}

where q_d is the desired trajectory, \tilde{q}=q-q_d, \Lambda>0 is a gain matrix, \dot{q}_r = \dot{q}_d - \Lambda \tilde{q}, \ddot{q}_r = \ddot{q}_d - \Lambda \dot{\tilde{q}}, and s = \dot{\tilde{q}} + \Lambda \tilde{q} = \dot{q} - \dot{q}_r is the sliding variable. K_d is a positive definite matrix. The regressor Y uses the reference velocity and acceleration—not the actual joint acceleration—making the law implementable with position and velocity sensors only. \hat{\theta} is an estimate of the health parameters, initialised at \theta_0.

6.2 Lyapunov Stability Proof

Substituting (11) into the dynamics (2) yields the closed‑loop error dynamics:

M(q)\dot{s} + C(q,\dot{q})s + K_d s = Y \tilde{\theta}
\tag{12}

with \tilde{\theta} = \theta - \hat{\theta}. Consider the Lyapunov function candidate:

V = \frac{1}{2} s^\top M(q) s + \frac{1}{2} \tilde{\theta}^\top \Gamma^{-1} \tilde{\theta}
\tag{13}

where \Gamma=\Gamma^\top >0. Differentiating along the trajectory:

\dot{V} = s^\top M\dot{s} + \frac{1}{2} s^\top \dot{M} s + \tilde{\theta}^\top \Gamma^{-1} ( -\dot{\hat{\theta}} )

Using (12) to replace M\dot{s} and the skew‑symmetry property \dot{M} = C + C^\top (which gives s^\top (\dot{M} - 2C)s = 0), we obtain:

\dot{V} = -s^\top K_d s + s^\top Y \tilde{\theta} - \tilde{\theta}^\top \Gamma^{-1} \dot{\hat{\theta}}

Choose the adaptation law:

\dot{\hat{\theta}} = \Gamma Y^\top s
\tag{14}

Then the last two terms cancel, giving \dot{V} = -s^\top K_d s \le 0. Hence V is non‑increasing, implying s,\tilde{\theta} \in \mathcal{L}_\infty. Since \dot{V} is uniformly continuous, Barbalat’s lemma implies s \to 0 as t\to\infty; i.e., asymptotic tracking.

Handling parameter jumps. If \theta undergoes a sudden jump, V experiences a bounded increase because \tilde{\theta} changes instantly, but thereafter (14) ensures \dot{V}\le 0 again, and the tracking error converges without instability. This is the formal foundation of graceful degradation stability.

6.3 Integration with Residual Learner

In the full NMR, the adaptive controller (11) provides a stability backbone. The residual network \Delta\pi_\psi essentially learns to generate the term -Y\hat{\theta} (and possibly higher‑order compensation) without explicit regressor construction. The Lyapunov argument guarantees that even if the residual output is imperfect, the overall system remains bounded as long as the residual is not persistently destabilizing—a condition enforced by the Lipschitz constraint and the watchdog.

