7. Handling Structural Failures: Behaviour Repertoire and Null‑Space Limping

7.1 Pre‑Computed Behaviour Library

For severe degradation where local torque redistribution cannot preserve task feasibility (e.g., complete loss of a leg, camera failure), the robot must switch to a qualitatively different strategy. We define a behaviour repertoire \mathcal{B} = \{\pi_k\}_{k=1}^N, each a control policy pre‑designed (or evolved via Quality‑Diversity algorithms) for a specific health descriptor \mathbf{d}_k\in\mathbb{R}^{d_h}. The descriptors sample the space of possible global health embeddings (which joints degraded, to what degree). At runtime, after each GNN update, the system computes the cosine similarity between the current \mathbf{h}^{\text{global}} and all \mathbf{d}_k; if the best match exceeds a threshold and the current task performance is poor, the active policy is switched to \pi_k. The residual network continues to fine‑tune the new policy.

7.2 Null‑Space Projection for Seized Joints

When a joint seizes completely (h_i=0), it must be excluded from the active torque set. Let \mathcal{S}\subset\{1,\dots,m\} be the set of controllable joints, and J_{\mathcal{S}}(q) the corresponding reduced Jacobian. The torque command for healthy joints is obtained by projecting the desired task force into the column space of J_{\mathcal{S}} and adding a null‑space term that explicitly protects the seized joint:

\tau_{\mathcal{S}} = J_{\mathcal{S}}^\dagger f_{\text{des}} + (I - J_{\mathcal{S}}^\dagger J_{\mathcal{S}}) z
\tag{15}

where J_{\mathcal{S}}^\dagger is the Moore‑Penrose pseudoinverse, and

z = -\gamma \, J_{\text{failed}}^\top f_{\text{contact}}
\tag{16}

Here J_{\text{failed}} is the Jacobian row for the seized joint, and f_{\text{contact}} is the estimated reaction force at that joint, derived from tendon strain via Hooke’s law: f_{\text{contact}} = k_t \varepsilon_i \,\mathbf{n}_i, where \mathbf{n}_i is the joint axis. Tendon strain \varepsilon_i is part of the node feature vector \mathbf{f}_i, making z a differentiable function of the health state. This construction mathematically enforces “intelligent limping”: the robot continues to track the desired end‑effector motion while explicitly minimising stress on the broken limb.

Design principle of intelligent limping: All failure‑mode adaptations are chosen so that the robot’s task value decays slower than the hardware degradation—i.e., graceful degradation in the true sense.

