9. Falsifiable Claims and Validation Design

This thesis presents no physical experiments; rather, it delivers a provably falsifiable specification. Every theoretical claim is mapped to a concrete validation artifact and a measurable metric, ready for instantiation in simulation (MuJoCo or equivalent) or on physical hardware. The Graceful Degradation Index (GDI) quantifies the depth‑of‑grace, making NMR measurable and falsifiable.

Graceful Degradation Index (GDI):

\text{GDI} = \frac{1}{T_{\text{end}}-T_{\text{fault}}} \int_{T_{\text{fault}}}^{T_{\text{end}}} \frac{P(t)}{P_{\text{nom}}} dt
\tag{17}
\]  

where P(t) is the task performance (tracking accuracy, manipulation success) and P_{\text{nom}} is the pre‑fault average. A complementary metric, Catastrophic Failure Rate (CFR), counts the fraction of trials resulting in emergency stop or fall. NMR’s theoretical targets: GDI > 0.85, CFR = 0.

Time‑to‑Adaptation (TTA₈₀): First instant after fault where P(t) \ge 0.8 P_{\text{nom}}. Theoretical prediction: TTA₈₀ < 3 s for novel actuator faults.

