10. Conclusions and Broader Impact

This dissertation establishes the mathematical and architectural foundations for the first general algorithmic self‑repair framework satisfying REPAIR‑ROBOT‑2026‑015. The core insight is that hardware degradation must be treated as a continuous, differentiable, first‑class state—not an exception. The specific theoretical contributions are:

1. A Lipschitz‑bounded GNN health‑perception layer with guaranteed sensitivity limits.
2. An analytically solvable, health‑weighted convex QP for real‑time torque redistribution.
3. An online residual learning scheme with meta‑initialisation and formal stability constraints.
4. A Lyapunov‑based adaptive controller proving asymptotic tracking under unknown continuous degradation using only position/velocity feedback.
5. A null‑space limping operator that explicitly minimises stress on broken joints, formally encoding “intelligent limping”.
6. A behavior‑repertoire selection mechanism driven by continuous health similarity.
7. A fully specified real‑time algorithm with proven sub‑50 ms latency on embedded hardware.

Broader significance. NMR addresses the foundational fragility of embodied AI: intelligent agents cannot yet inhabit physical bodies for months or years because hardware degrades and control stacks break. By making the health state a differentiable, controllable variable, NMR provides the missing link between high‑level planning and the physics of actuators. A robot that can limp is a robot that can learn longer, explore further, and serve as a durable vessel for autonomous intelligence. Economically, preventing unnecessary emergency stops saves fleets millions annually. NMR also provides the “body‑aware” layer that future multi‑agent systems will need to share degradation models across morphologies.

Risk and mitigation. Three main risks are identified and addressed architecturally: (i) GNN false‑positive health drop → unnecessary limping, mitigated by the Lipschitz bound and a human‑in‑the‑loop override on WQP constraints; (ii) residual learner destabilisation during a jump fault, mitigated by meta‑initialisation and the watchdog‑triggered Lyapunov backup controller; (iii) 50 ms latency not met on ultra‑low‑power hardware, mitigated by a fallback to quantised spiking neural network inference for the residual while the WQP solve remains <1 ms.


