Appendix B: Full Proofs of Lipschitz Lemmas

B.1 Lemma 1 — Lipschitz Health Embedding

Statement. Assume the GNN of Eq. (4) with layer count L satisfies:
• \|W^{(l)}\|_2 \le 1 for all l (spectral normalisation),
• Activation function \sigma = \text{ReLU} (1‑Lipschitz),
• Aggregation weights \alpha_{ij}^{(l)} \ge 0 and \sum_{j \in \mathcal{N}(i)} \alpha_{ij}^{(l)} \le 1 for all i, l,
• READOUT is a mean‑pooling plus linear map with operator norm \le 1.
Then the mapping \mathbf{f} \mapsto \mathbf{h}^{\text{global}} is L_h-Lipschitz with L_h \le 2^L.

Proof.
Let \mathbf{f} = \{\mathbf{f}_i\}_{i=1}^N and \tilde{\mathbf{f}} = \{\tilde{\mathbf{f}}_i\}_{i=1}^N be two sets of node features. Define \mathbf{z}_i^{(0)} = \mathbf{f}_i, \tilde{\mathbf{z}}_i^{(0)} = \tilde{\mathbf{f}}_i. Denote d^{(l)} = \max_i \|\mathbf{z}_i^{(l)} - \tilde{\mathbf{z}}_i^{(l)}\|. We prove by induction that d^{(l+1)} \le 2 d^{(l)}.

For any node i,

\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{z}_i^{(l+1)} &= \sigma\!\left( W^{(l)} \mathbf{z}_i^{(l)} + \sum_{j\in\mathcal{N}(i)} \alpha_{ij}^{(l)} \mathbf{z}_j^{(l)} \right), \\
\tilde{\mathbf{z}}_i^{(l+1)} &= \sigma\!\left( W^{(l)} \tilde{\mathbf{z}}_i^{(l)} + \sum_{j\in\mathcal{N}(i)} \alpha_{ij}^{(l)} \tilde{\mathbf{z}}_j^{(l)} \right).
\end{aligned}

Since \sigma is 1‑Lipschitz,

\begin{aligned}
\|\mathbf{z}_i^{(l+1)} - \tilde{\mathbf{z}}_i^{(l+1)}\| &\le \left\| W^{(l)}(\mathbf{z}_i^{(l)} - \tilde{\mathbf{z}}_i^{(l)}) + \sum_{j} \alpha_{ij}^{(l)} (\mathbf{z}_j^{(l)} - \tilde{\mathbf{z}}_j^{(l)}) \right\| \\
&\le \|W^{(l)}\| \cdot \|\mathbf{z}_i^{(l)} - \tilde{\mathbf{z}}_i^{(l)}\| + \sum_{j} \alpha_{ij}^{(l)} \|\mathbf{z}_j^{(l)} - \tilde{\mathbf{z}}_j^{(l)}\| \\
&\le 1 \cdot d^{(l)} + d^{(l)} \sum_{j} \alpha_{ij}^{(l)} \\
&\le d^{(l)} + d^{(l)} = 2 d^{(l)}.
\end{aligned}

Thus d^{(l+1)} \le 2 d^{(l)}, and after L layers, d^{(L)} \le 2^L d^{(0)}.

The READOUT function first computes the mean node embedding \bar{\mathbf{z}} = \frac{1}{N}\sum_i \mathbf{z}_i^{(L)}, then applies a linear map W_{\text{out}} with \|W_{\text{out}}\|_2 \le 1. The mean operation is 1‑Lipschitz with respect to the max‑norm (if one node changes by \delta, the mean changes by \delta/N \le \delta). Applying W_{\text{out}} preserves the bound. Hence

\|\mathbf{h}^{\text{global}} - \tilde{\mathbf{h}}^{\text{global}}\| \le d^{(L)} \le 2^L d^{(0)} = 2^L \max_i \|\mathbf{f}_i - \tilde{\mathbf{f}}_i\|.

Therefore L_h \le 2^L. ∎

