B.2 Lemma 2 — Bounded Torque Sensitivity

Statement. Given h_i \ge h_{\min} > 0 for all i, and assuming J(q) is full‑row‑rank with bounded Jacobian condition number, the solution map \tau^*: \mathbf{h} \mapsto \tau^*(\mathbf{h}) of the QP (5) is Lipschitz continuous. There exists a constant L_\tau such that \|\tau^*(\mathbf{h}) - \tau^*(\tilde{\mathbf{h}})\| \le L_\tau \|\mathbf{h} - \tilde{\mathbf{h}}\|.

Proof.
Consider the QP in standard form: \min_\tau \frac{1}{2} \tau^\top P(\mathbf{h}) \tau + q^\top \tau subject to A\tau \le b, where P(\mathbf{h}) = J^\top J + W(\mathbf{h}) + \beta \Upsilon. Because h_i \ge h_{\min}, the diagonal entries of W are bounded: w_i \in [\underline{w}, \overline{w}] with \underline{w} = w_{\max}/(1+\epsilon), \overline{w} = w_{\max}/(h_{\min}+\epsilon). Hence P(\mathbf{h}) is positive definite with eigenvalues bounded uniformly away from zero, and its inverse is smooth in \mathbf{h}. The unconstrained solution is \tau_{\text{unc}} = P(\mathbf{h})^{-1} c (with c = J^\top f_{\text{des}}), which is differentiable and thus Lipschitz on the compact domain of \mathbf{h}.

When constraints are active, the solution operator of a strictly convex QP with polyhedral constraints is piecewise affine in the problem data (a classical result from parametric quadratic programming). The gradient of the solution with respect to the objective parameters is bounded because the active‑set manifold changes only at finitely many points where the active set changes; in each region, the solution is given by the KKT conditions:

\tau^* = P^{-1}(c - A_{\mathcal{A}}^\top \lambda^*), \quad \lambda^* = (A_{\mathcal{A}} P^{-1} A_{\mathcal{A}}^\top)^{-1} (b_{\mathcal{A}} - A_{\mathcal{A}} P^{-1} c),

where \mathcal{A} is the active constraint set. Since P^{-1} depends smoothly on W and W depends smoothly on \mathbf{h}, each piece is Lipschitz. The global Lipschitz constant L_\tau is the maximum over all pieces. Therefore \tau^*(\mathbf{h}) is Lipschitz. ∎

