Appendix C: Real‑Time QP Solver with Active‑Set Warm‑Start

The health‑weighted QP (5) must be solved in <5 ms, even when box constraints are active. This appendix details the analytical unconstrained solution and the primal‑dual active‑set algorithm used for constraint satisfaction.

C.1 Unconstrained Solution

Given P = J^\top J + W + \beta\Upsilon \in \mathbb{R}^{m\times m} symmetric positive definite, and c = J^\top f_{\text{des}}, the unconstrained minimizer is \tau^* = P^{-1} c. Because W is diagonal and J^\top J is dense, we pre‑compute the Cholesky decomposition of P at startup and update it via rank‑r adjustments (using the Sherman‑Morrison‑Woodbury formula) only when a health weight w_i changes by more than a threshold (e.g., 5%). For a typical 30‑DOF robot, a full Cholesky takes ~0.1 ms; the diag‑only update is O(m^2).

C.2 Primal‑Dual Active‑Set Algorithm

For constrained QP: \min_\tau \frac{1}{2} \tau^\top P \tau - c^\top \tau s.t. \tau_{\min} \le \tau \le \tau_{\max}. We initialise with the unconstrained solution \tau^{(0)} = \tau^*, and identify the set of violated bounds \mathcal{W} = \{i \mid \tau_i < \tau_{\min,i} \text{ or } \tau_i > \tau_{\max,i}\}. We then enforce equality constraints for those variables: for i \in \mathcal{W}, set \tau_i = \tau_{\min,i} if violated below, or \tau_{\max,i} if above, and solve the reduced system for the remaining free variables:

P_{\text{free,free}} \tau_{\text{free}} = c_{\text{free}} - P_{\text{free,fixed}} \tau_{\text{fixed}}.

We iterate, moving variables in and out of the active set based on Lagrange multiplier signs (dual feasibility). Because the problem is strictly convex, convergence to the global optimum is guaranteed. The number of active‑set changes is typically 2–3 per solve; with warm‑start (using the previous solution’s active set), the total time remains <5 ms on ARM Cortex‑A72.

