Appendix F: Graph Neural Network Architecture Detail

The GNN responsible for \mathbf{h}^{\text{global}} is designed to be both expressive and Lipschitz‑bounded.

· Input features: per node: [h_i, \dot{h}_i, \tau_i^{\text{err}}, \varepsilon_i, T_i, \text{vib}_i] (7 features). \dot{h}_i is computed via a simple finite difference.
· Graph construction: Edges exist between joints that share a rigid link (kinematic graph) and between joints on the same limb (dynamic coupling). Edge attributes are the Euclidean distance in joint space.
· Architecture:
  · Layer 1: 7 → 64, spectral normalised, \alpha_{ij} = \text{softmax}( \text{MLP}(\mathbf{z}_i, \mathbf{z}_j, \text{dist}_{ij}) ).
  · Layer 2: 64 → 64, same structure.
  · Layer 3: 64 → 32, same structure.
  · Layer 4 (output per node): 32 → 16.
  · Global readout: mean pooling + Linear(16, 32) → \mathbf{h}^{\text{global}} (32‑dim).
· Total parameters: ~40 K.
· Activation: ReLU for all internal layers; readout has no activation (linear).
· Normalisation: Weight matrices constrained to \|W\|_2 \le 1 via power iteration spectral normalisation, updated every forward pass with negligible overhead. Aggregation weights normalised via softmax.

The GNN runs at 1 kHz on the target hardware within 3 ms due to its modest size and the use of single‑precision floating point.

